OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F315A1785444469
OL,3,CInterest rates held at 3.75% as Bank
OL,4,Cof England warns of inflation rise
OL,6,FThe Bank of England has held interest
OL,7,Frates for the fifth successive time
OL,8,Fdespite cautioning that energy prices
OL,9,Fand an AI-driven memory chip shortage
OL,10,Fwill drive inflation higher.
OL,12,FForecasts by the central bank
OL,13,Fsuggested food inflation is set to
OL,14,Frise again later this year as supply
OL,15,Fissues linked to hot and dry weather
OL,16,Faffected prices for shoppers.
OL,18,FA majority of the central bank's nine-
OL,19,Fstrong Monetary Policy Committee (MPC)
OL,20,Fvoted in favour of keeping interest
OL,21,Frates at 3.75%.
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G1/9
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!,.!T,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,23,TppppppppppppppppppGsource: itv.com/news
OL,24,ANext Page BNat News CNat TV FClean Feed
FL,316,300,200,140,000,100
PN,31502
SC,0002
CT,2,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F315A1785444469
OL,3,CInterest rates held at 3.75% as Bank
OL,4,Cof England warns of inflation rise
OL,6,FThis is the first monetary policy
OL,7,Freport since Andy Burnham became prime
OL,8,Fminister last week.
OL,10,FPolicymakers warned that the war in
OL,11,Fthe Middle East is still causing
OL,12,Fsignificant uncertainty over the
OL,13,Fglobal economy.
OL,15,FThe Bank predicted that UK inflation
OL,16,Fwill average around 3% this year,
OL,17,Fbefore slowing to 2.7% in 2027 and
OL,18,F1.8% in 2028.
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G2/9
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!,.!T,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,23,TppppppppppppppppppGsource: itv.com/news
OL,24,ANext Page BNat News CNat TV FClean Feed
FL,316,300,200,140,000,100
PN,31503
SC,0003
CT,2,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F315A1785444469
OL,3,CInterest rates held at 3.75% as Bank
OL,4,Cof England warns of inflation rise
OL,6,FAndrew Bailey, Governor of the Bank of
OL,7,FEngland, said: "Inflation has fallen
OL,8,Ffaster than we'd expected, but the
OL,9,Fconflict in the Middle East continues
OL,10,Fto mean high and volatile energy
OL,11,Fprices.
OL,13,F"That will cause inflation to rise
OL,14,Fagain later this year.
OL,16,F"However the conflict unfolds, our job
OL,17,Fis to make sure any increase in
OL,18,Finflation is temporary and that it
OL,19,Fcomes back to our 2% target."
OL,26,@oD@POUuD@AOUxD@AOU{D@]O]
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G3/9
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!,.!T,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,23,TppppppppppppppppppGsource: itv.com/news
OL,24,ANext Page BNat News CNat TV FClean Feed
FL,316,300,200,140,000,100
PN,31504
SC,0004
CT,2,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F315A1785444469
OL,3,CInterest rates held at 3.75% as Bank
OL,4,Cof England warns of inflation rise
OL,6,FThe Bank also suggested that consumer
OL,7,Fprice inflation ` recorded at 2.6%
OL,8,Flast month ` is likely to peak around
OL,9,F3.2% later this year, before steadily
OL,10,Feasing back towards the Bank's 2%
OL,11,Finflation target.
OL,13,FIn last month's MPC meeting, the Bank
OL,14,Fhad suggested inflation would peak
OL,15,F"slightly above 3.25%".
OL,17,FHigher oil prices linked to the Middle
OL,18,FEast conflict are expected to be the
OL,19,Fmain driver of inflation, pushing
OL,20,Fenergy prices higher for households
OL,21,Fand businesses in the coming months.
OL,26,@wD@AOUwD@VO]
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G4/9
